course: books
lesson: 4
title: Alice in Wonderland, chapter II, part 2: The Pool of Tears and the Mouse
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Alice notices she has slipped on one of the Rabbit's tiny gloves: she is shrinking again, and fast, because of the fan in her hand, so she drops it just in time.
explain: She runs to the little door, but it is shut again and the key is back on the table, and then she slips and lands up to her chin in salt water: the pool of her own tears.
explain: A mouse is swimming there too, and Alice politely tries English, then her school French, but the first French sentence she knows is "Where is my cat?", which terrifies it.
explain: Every time she tries to make friends she makes it worse, chatting happily about her cat Dinah and a rat-killing dog, until the Mouse swims away offended.
explain: She calls it back kindly, it promises to tell her why it hates cats and dogs, and Alice leads a crowd of soggy birds and animals to the shore: a funny, slightly awkward scene about putting your foot in it.
fact: Alice finds she has put on one of the Rabbit's little white kid gloves, which tells her she is growing small again.
fact: Measuring herself by the table she is about two feet high and still shrinking; the fan is the cause, and she drops it "just in time to avoid shrinking away altogether".
fact: She calls it "a narrow escape", meaning a near miss with danger.
fact: The little door is shut again and the golden key lies on the glass table as before, so "things are worse than ever".
fact: Her foot slips and "splash! she was up to her chin in salt water"; she first thinks it is the sea and that she can "go back by railway".
fact: From one trip to the seaside Alice believes every English beach has bathing machines (little huts on wheels that were rolled into the sea for people to change in), children digging with wooden spades, lodging houses and a railway station.
fact: It is really "the pool of tears which she had wept when she was nine feet high", and she says "I wish I hadn't cried so much!", afraid she will be "drowned in my own tears".
fact: At first she thinks the splashing creature is a walrus or hippopotamus, but, being so small, she sees it is only a mouse.
fact: Alice says "O Mouse!" because she once saw in her brother's Latin Grammar "A mouse—of a mouse—to a mouse—a mouse—O mouse!"; "O Mouse" is the old way of calling to someone.
fact: The Mouse says nothing to her English, so Alice thinks "Perhaps it doesn't understand English" and guesses it is a French mouse "come over with William the Conqueror", although she has no clear idea how long ago history happened (William came in 1066).
fact: She says "Où est ma chatte?", the first sentence in her French book, which means "Where is my cat?"; the Mouse leaps out of the water in fright.
fact: The Mouse cries, "Would you like cats if you were me?" and calls cats "nasty, low, vulgar things"; "vulgar" here means common and rude.
fact: When Alice says "We won't talk about her any more", the Mouse cries "We indeed!", offended at being counted in, as if it would ever talk about cats.
fact: Alice then praises a farmer's terrier that "kills all the rats" (the farmer says it is "worth a hundred pounds"), and the Mouse swims away making "quite a commotion" (a lot of noisy fuss).
fact: When Alice promises not to talk about cats or dogs, the Mouse comes back, its face "quite pale (with passion, Alice thought)", and says it will tell its "history" on the shore.
fact: The pool has become crowded with "a Duck and a Dodo, a Lory and an Eaglet": a Lory is a small bright parrot, an Eaglet is a young eagle, and the Dodo is a big bird that died out long ago.
fact: Old words in today's English: "I daresay" = I suppose, "take a fancy to" = start to like, "a capital one for" = really good at, "with passion" = with strong anger.
exercise: What did Alice notice she had put on while talking to herself? | One of the Rabbit's little white kid gloves | A pair of her sister's warm woolly mittens | The Rabbit's waistcoat with the watch in it | A crown that had fallen from the Duchess
exercise: Why did the glove on her hand tell Alice she was shrinking? | It was a tiny glove and now it fitted her | It was tight and squeezing her fingers hard | It had "SHRINK ME" written on it in ink | The Rabbit had told her what the glove did
exercise: About how tall was Alice when she measured herself by the table? | About two feet | About nine feet | About ten inches | About four feet
exercise: What was making Alice shrink this time? | The fan she was holding | The little kid glove on her hand | The salt water | The golden key
exercise: What did Alice do just in time? | Dropped the fan | Ate the cake | Drank the bottle | Grabbed the key
exercise: Here "a narrow escape" means... | A near miss with danger | A very thin door to run through | A quick way out of a tunnel | A small hole in a garden wall
exercise: Why were things "worse than ever" when she got back to the little door? | It was shut and the key was on the table again | The garden had disappeared behind a brick wall | The Rabbit was standing guard in front of it | The door had grown far too tall for her to open
exercise: What happened when Alice's foot slipped? | She landed up to her chin in salt water | She fell back down another deep rabbit-hole | She tumbled into the lovely garden at last | She dropped the golden key into a crack
exercise: What did Alice first think the water was? | The sea | A bath | A river | A well
exercise: How did Alice think she could get home from the sea? | By railway | By boat | By carriage | On foot
exercise: What is a bathing machine? | A hut on wheels rolled into the sea for changing | A big machine that fills a bath with hot water | A kind of boat that children rowed along the shore | A pump that sprayed sea water over the beach
exercise: What was the water really? | The pool of tears she had wept when nine feet high | A pond that the White Rabbit had dug for the Duchess | Rain that had poured in through the roof of the hall | Water spilt from the bottle marked "DRINK ME"
exercise: What did Alice first think the splashing creature was? | A walrus or hippopotamus | A whale or a shark | A crocodile or a frog | A duck or a swan
exercise: What was the creature really? | A mouse | A rat | A frog | A little fish
exercise: Where had Alice seen "O mouse!"? | In her brother's Latin Grammar | In her sister's picture book | On a big painted sign at the seaside | In the Rabbit's letter
exercise: Why did Alice think the Mouse might be French? | It did not answer her English | It had a little French flag | It was eating French bread | It said "bonjour" to her
exercise: Who did Alice think the Mouse came over with? | William the Conqueror | The White Rabbit and the Duchess | A French farmer and his dog | The Queen of England and her court
exercise: What does "Où est ma chatte?" mean? | Where is my cat? | Where is my house? | What is your name? | How are you today?
exercise: Why did the Mouse leap out of the water? | Alice had asked about a cat | Alice had splashed it | It saw a hungry dog | It heard the White Rabbit coming
exercise: What did the Mouse call cats? | "nasty, low, vulgar things" | "dear quiet things" | "the best cats in the world" | "capital ones for catching mice"
exercise: What did Alice tell the Mouse about the farmer's terrier? | It kills all the rats | It is afraid of mice | It sleeps all day long | It belongs to the Queen
exercise: How much did the farmer say his dog was worth? | A hundred pounds | A thousand pounds | Ten pounds | A penny
exercise: What did the Mouse promise to tell on the shore? | Its history, and why it hates cats and dogs | The way to the lovely garden behind the door | Where the White Rabbit had gone to hide | How Alice could get back to her normal size
exercise: Which birds were named in the crowded pool? | A Duck, a Dodo, a Lory and an Eaglet | A Swan, a Goose, a Hen and a Robin | An Owl, a Crow, a Gull and a Parrot | A Pigeon, a Magpie, a Canary and a Crab
exercise: What is a Lory? | A small bright parrot | A young eagle | A big bird that died out | A kind of duck
exercise: Here "vulgar" means... | Common and rude | Very clever and wise | Shy and quiet | Old and wise
exercise: Thinking back: whose gloves and fan were these, and why had he dropped them? | The Rabbit's, dropped when giant Alice spoke to him | The Duchess's, left on the table for the Rabbit | Alice's own, which she brought down the hole | Dinah's, lost while she was chasing a bat
exercise: Thinking back: when did Alice first mention Dinah in the book? | While she was falling down the well | When she met the Mouse in the pool | When she found the golden key | On the bank before the Rabbit came
test: Lily goes: "Alice dropped the fan 'cos her arm got tired." Put her right. | She dropped it because it was making her shrink away | She dropped it because the Rabbit asked for it back | She dropped it because it fell into the salty pool | She dropped it because it was far too heavy for her
test: Jayden, Gen Alpha: "Alice talking about her cat to a MOUSE? That's not the rizz, bro." ("rizz" = charm, being good at making friends.) Why was it a bad idea? | Cats catch and eat mice, so the Mouse was terrified | The Mouse was allergic to fur and sneezed at once | Mice cannot understand English or French at all | The Mouse had a pet cat of its own at home already
test: Why is it funny that Alice says "O Mouse!"? | She uses an old school-book way of speaking to a mouse | Mice in England are always called "O" by children | It is the name of the Mouse's family in French | She is trying to sing the Mouse a little song
test: What would have happened if Alice had kept holding the fan? | She might have shrunk away altogether | She would have grown back to nine feet tall | Nothing, because the fan had no magic at all | She would have floated up to the roof of the hall
test: Grandpa, Black Country: "Her's bostin', that Alice, but her talks too much about that cat." ("bostin'" = great.) What does Alice keep doing to the Mouse? | Upsetting it by talking about cats and dogs | Splashing water over its head and whiskers | Asking it the way to the garden again and again | Trying to pick it up and put it in her pocket
test: True or false: Alice knew exactly when William the Conqueror lived. | False: she had no clear idea how long ago anything happened | True: she had learnt the date in her French lessons | True: she had met him once on a trip to the seaside | False: she had never heard his name before that day
test: Sophie, sarky: "Oh well done Alice, cry a whole lake and then fall in it." What is she pointing out? | Alice nearly drowns in her own tears | Alice made the lake on purpose to swim | Alice's sister filled the pool with water | The Rabbit pushed Alice into the pool
test: Why does Alice say "I wish I hadn't cried so much!"? | She is now swimming in her own tears | Her eyes are sore and red from crying | The Rabbit told her off for crying | Her sister will be cross with her
test: Why did Alice's view of the seaside make her think she could go home by railway? | She believed every English beach had a railway station | She could see a train passing high above the pool | The Mouse told her a train would come along soon | She remembered taking a train down the rabbit-hole
test: What does the Mouse's "Would you like cats if you were me?" ask Alice to do? | Imagine how things look to a mouse | Go home and bring Dinah to meet it | Answer a question in French this time | Stop swimming and climb out of the pool
test: Looking back: Alice has now been tiny twice. What shrank her the first time, back in chapter I? | The bottle marked "DRINK ME" | The Rabbit's fan | The cake marked "EAT ME" | The salt water
test: Looking back: in chapter I Alice said "Antipathies" instead of "Antipodes". What does she muddle in this part? | When William the Conqueror lived | The colour of the Rabbit's eyes | The name of her own cat | Which hand the glove is on
test: How is Alice different with the Mouse from how she was alone in the hall? | She now has company and tries hard to make a friend | She is now angry and wants to frighten everybody | She is now giant and too big to talk to anyone | She has forgotten all about wanting to go home
test: Ciara from Cork: "The Mouse came back to her, grand, but sure it was pale as anything." ("grand" = fine, okay.) Why did Alice think it was pale? | With passion, meaning strong anger | With cold from the salty water | With joy at seeing her again | With hunger after the long swim
test: ★★★ The Latin Grammar line is "A mouse—of a mouse—to a mouse—a mouse—O mouse!" How many times does the word "mouse" appear in it? | Five | Four | Six | Three
test: ★★★ Alice was over nine feet tall when she cried the pool, which was four inches deep. Now she is so small that she is "up to her chin". What does that tell us? | She is now only a few inches tall | She is still about two feet tall | She is lying flat on the bottom | The pool has grown to nine feet deep
test: ★★★ Read carefully: Alice says "We won't talk about her any more". Who answers "We indeed!", and why? | The Mouse, offended at the word "we" | Alice, agreeing with herself | The Duck, speaking for all the birds | The Dodo, calling the meeting to order
exam: Identify the object whose retention was determined to be the cause of the protagonist's diminution on this occasion. | The fan | The glove | The key | The bottle
exam: Ascertain the true nature of the body of salt water into which the protagonist subsequently fell. | The pool of tears previously shed by herself | An arm of the sea upon the English coast | A reservoir constructed by the White Rabbit | A well connected to the centre of the earth
exam: It is given that the protagonist addressed the Mouse in the French language. Determine the meaning of the sentence employed. | "Where is my cat?" | "Where is the way out?" | "What is your name, sir?" | "Do you speak English?"
exam: Select the response most consistent with the established facts concerning the reason for which the Mouse repeatedly withdrew from the protagonist. | She spoke of animals that hunt mice | She splashed it repeatedly with water | She declined to accompany it to shore | She addressed it in the Latin language
